#!/bin/sh
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2025-2026 Tyrone Ross, Jr <46267523+tyroneross@users.noreply.github.com>
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Local pre-push gate — replaces the ubuntu GitHub Actions rally-gate.
#
# OPT-IN, not automatic. Git does not read this file unless
# `core.hooksPath` is pointed at `.githooks` — a per-clone, per-operator
# decision (`git config core.hooksPath .githooks`). Cloning this repo does
# NOT turn the hook on by itself; there is no trust-on-clone bootstrap here.
# Enabling it is a trust decision: once enabled, this hook executes shell
# code (scripts/run-quality-gate.sh, scripts/check-release-parity.sh) on
# every `git push`. See the ARP-006 note below for how that execution is
# scoped, and READ those two scripts yourself before you flip the switch —
# nothing about `core.hooksPath` audits their contents for you.
#
# Validates the EXACT objects being pushed, not the current (possibly dirty,
# possibly ahead-of/behind-the-push) working tree: for every distinct
# non-deletion `local_sha` git is about to push (computed by
# scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh from the ref-update tuples git supplies on
# stdin), this hook checks out a clean DETACHED worktree at that exact SHA
# so the gate exercises the pushed CONTENT. Detached worktrees are always
# torn down via a trap — success, gate failure, or interrupt — so a blocked
# push never leaves stray worktrees.
#
# ARP-006 (2026-08 third-party security audit, GitHub issue #52): the gate
# scripts THEMSELVES are not trusted just because they came from the pushed
# worktree — a pushed branch that edits scripts/run-quality-gate.sh or
# scripts/check-release-parity.sh would otherwise silently execute its own
# (possibly malicious) version of those scripts on this machine. Fix:
#   1. Pin: resolve both gate scripts from a REF, not a working-tree path —
#      ${RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF:-main} — on the theory that `main` is
#      reviewed/trusted and the pushed worktree is not.
#   2. Compare: diff the pinned copy against the pushed worktree's copy of
#      each script.
#        - Identical -> run the pinned copy (same bytes either way).
#        - Different -> REFUSE by default and print the diff. A pushed
#          change to the gate script itself never executes unreviewed. If
#          the diff is a legitimate in-flight edit to the gate (the push
#          IS the change), re-run with RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_GATE_CHANGE=1 to
#          explicitly accept running the pushed tree's version — an
#          operator decision, never a silent default.
#   3. No pin ref resolves at all (e.g. no `main` branch yet — bootstrap) ->
#      fall back to the pushed worktree's own copy, loudly, since there is
#      nothing else to pin to.
#   Either way, the script that actually RUNS still `cd`s into the pushed
#   worktree (both gate scripts resolve their own repo_root via
#   `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`), so the SUBJECT under test is always
#   the pushed content — only the gate CODE's trust source changes.
#
#   WHAT THE PIN DOES NOT COVER. The pin protects three DISPATCHER scripts,
#   plus one compare-only path (see ARP-R-05 below). It does not, and cannot,
#   protect the code those dispatchers go on to execute out of the pushed
#   tree, which by design includes: every `cargo test` body, `build.rs`, and
#   proc macro that run-quality-gate.sh compiles and runs;
#   scripts/generate_host_surfaces.py and the tests/scripts/test_*.py unittest
#   modules that check-release-parity.sh invokes;
#   scripts/build-codex-artifact.sh; and every tests/hooks/test_*.sh the parity
#   script globs. Running pushed code is the POINT of a gate — the pin only
#   decides who dispatches it. Read the end-of-run "gate-script pin comparison
#   complete" summary as "these named paths were diffed against the pin", never
#   as "nothing unreviewed will execute".
#
# RC-034 (2026-08 independent security review; docs/ROOT-CAUSE-REGISTER.md):
# the gap above was exploitable through a file the pin never looked at. A push
# that ADDS tests/hooks/test_zz_anything.sh touches none of the three pinned
# names, so the pin reports healthy, and the PINNED check-release-parity.sh
# then globs and executes the new attacker-supplied file. Two changes:
#   a. This hook exports the resolved pin as RALLY_PREPUSH_PIN_COMMIT.
#      check-release-parity.sh uses it to list the host tests that existed at
#      the pin and REFUSES any tests/hooks/test_*.sh in the pushed tree that
#      is absent from, or differs from, that list. Override per the same
#      idiom: RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_UNPINNED_HOST_TEST=1. When the variable is
#      unset — CI, the release workflow, a manual run — the parity script
#      behaves exactly as before, because there is no pin to compare against.
#      This NARROWS the pushed-code surface by one entry point. It does not
#      eliminate it: the cargo, python, and build-codex-artifact.sh paths
#      listed above still execute pushed content, unpinned.
#   b. The vacuity check (SEC-005a) only catches an env pin aimed at a commit
#      IN this push. An env pin aimed at any other attacker-controlled ref —
#      a local `evil` branch carrying the same malicious gate scripts as the
#      pushed branch — resolves fine, is not in the push, and used to pass
#      silently while `diff -q` reported the pinned and pushed copies
#      identical. Content-identity was being read as evidence of review. Since
#      SEC-005's premise is that the environment is not trusted, a RESOLVING
#      env-supplied pin now requires RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_ENV_PIN=1 in EVERY
#      case, not only the vacuous one. The DEFAULT `main` pin is unchanged.
#      An env pin that does NOT resolve still takes the bootstrap fallback
#      below (loud warning, pushed-tree gate scripts, no ack) — that path is
#      unchanged and remains a way to run the three dispatchers unpinned.
#
# SEC-005 (2026-08 review of the ARP-006 fix): the pin ref is read from the
# environment, and the environment is not trusted. Anything running as this UID
# — a committed `.envrc`, a Makefile, an npm script — can set
# RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF=HEAD. The pinned copy is then byte-identical to
# the pushed copy, `diff -q` says identical, the pushed branch's own gate script
# runs, and the hook used to print an affirmative "gate scripts pinned to
# HEAD @ <sha>" with no warning at all. The pin became a no-op that reported
# success. Two changes:
#   a. VACUITY CHECK. After the pin resolves, its commit is compared against
#      every SHA being pushed. Equal means the pin reviewed nothing. The hook
#      prints pin_commit and each pushed SHA side by side, always.
#   b. An env-SUPPLIED pin that turns out vacuous is REFUSED (override with
#      RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN=1). A DEFAULT pin that is vacuous — you are
#      pushing `main` itself, where the pin cannot be independent by
#      construction — used to warn loudly and continue. SUPERSEDED by ARP-R-05a
#      below: it is now refused behind the same ack, because "warns and
#      continues" on the repo's normal path is not a control.
#   The pushed SHAs for that check are extracted from the raw stdin tuples with
#   awk, NOT with scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh: the check decides whether the
#   pin is trustworthy, so it must not depend on a script the pushed branch
#   could have edited.
#
# SEC-005 (second half): scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh is itself a gate script
# — it runs from the working tree, before any other gate, and it decides which
# SHAs get validated. It is now pinned exactly like the other two, so the pin
# block below runs BEFORE the parser is invoked.
#
# ARP-R-05 (2026-08 independent security re-assessment;
# docs/ROOT-CAUSE-REGISTER.md): the gate reported success on a path that
# reviewed nothing. Three defects, all in "what the operator is told versus what
# was actually compared":
#   a. THE DEFAULT PIN'S VACUITY CHECK ONLY WARNED. SEC-005b refused a vacuous
#      ENV pin but let a vacuous DEFAULT pin continue with a warning, on the
#      reasoning that refusing would block every push of the pin branch. But
#      pushing `main` while the pin IS `main` is this repo's NORMAL path: the
#      warning fired on essentially every push, the gate scripts in the push
#      were compared against themselves, and the hook went on to execute the
#      pushed tree's code anyway. A control that never fires on the path
#      everyone uses is not a control; it is a log line. The default case is now
#      REFUSED behind the ack that already gated the env case
#      (RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN=1). It is a deliberate operator decision
#      per push of the pin branch, not a silent default — and it is a two-word
#      prefix, not a workflow change.
#   b. THE AFFIRMATIVE PIN LINE PRINTED BEFORE ANY COMPARISON. "gate scripts
#      pinned to <ref> @ <sha>" used to print immediately after `git show`
#      copied the pinned bytes out — before resolve_gate_script had diffed
#      anything, and before the vacuity check ran. A reader saw an affirmative
#      statement about a comparison that had not happened. Now: a NEUTRAL
#      "pin ref resolves to <sha>; nothing compared yet" marker at that point,
#      and the affirmative line moved to AFTER the last comparison, where it
#      enumerates each path, which copy ran (pinned vs pushed), and why. The
#      end-of-run line likewise no longer says "all gates green"; it names the
#      SHA count and the two dispatchers that exited 0, and restates that the
#      unpinned code they dispatched is not covered by any of it.
#   c. hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh WAS EXECUTED BY PINNED TESTS BUT NOT PINNED.
#      The pin resolved a hardcoded `scripts/<name>` prefix, so only files under
#      scripts/ could ever join the set. tests/hooks/test_no_autoprovision.sh
#      and tests/hooks/test_ensure_rally_binary.sh both EXECUTE
#      hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh, and that file contains `curl`, `chmod +x`,
#      and `cargo install`. RC-034 pinned the test files themselves — so a push
#      that left every pinned test byte-identical while editing the engine those
#      tests invoke reached execution unreviewed, with the hook reporting a
#      healthy pin. Fix: the pin is now keyed on repo-relative PATHS
#      (GATE_SCRIPT_PATHS), not bare names under scripts/, and
#      hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh is in the set as a COMPARE-ONLY entry — this
#      hook never dispatches it, so it gets the pinned-vs-pushed diff and the
#      same refusal, but no exec path. Pinned copies live in $pin_dir under a
#      flattened name (`/` -> `__`) so two paths can never collide there.
#
# Blocks the push on failure. Emergency override (skips the WHOLE hook,
# pin check included): RALLY_SKIP_PREPUSH=1 git push
#
# The actual gate steps (fmt/clippy/tests/doctests/audit) live in
# scripts/run-quality-gate.sh — ONE definition shared with CI
# (.github/workflows/ci.yml) so local and CI can never silently diverge.
# Keep the toolchain pin (RALLY_QG_TOOLCHAIN / rust-toolchain.toml) in
# lockstep across both.
set -e

if [ "${RALLY_SKIP_PREPUSH:-}" = "1" ]; then
  echo "pre-push: SKIPPED (RALLY_SKIP_PREPUSH=1)" >&2
  exit 0
fi

repo_root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)
cd "$repo_root"

# git supplies one "<local_ref> <local_sha> <remote_ref> <remote_sha>" tuple
# per line on stdin (see `git help githooks`, PRE-PUSH). Capture it once to a
# scratch file: scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh needs to read it, and stdin
# can only be consumed once.
stdin_capture=$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/rally-prepush-stdin.XXXXXX")
cat > "$stdin_capture"

sha_file=$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/rally-prepush-shas.XXXXXX")
# ARP-R-05b: every pinned-vs-pushed VERDICT is appended here as it is decided,
# and the whole file is printed once at the end. The affirmative pin statement
# therefore cannot be emitted before the comparisons it describes.
pin_summary=$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/rally-prepush-pinsummary.XXXXXX")
worktree_base=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/rally-prepush-wt.XXXXXX")
pin_dir=$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/rally-prepush-pin.XXXXXX")
cleanup() {
  # Runs on EVERY exit path (normal completion, a gate failure under `set -e`,
  # or an interrupt) — the single place worktrees + the pin scratch dir get
  # torn down, so a failed push never leaves one behind.
  if [ -d "$worktree_base" ]; then
    for d in "$worktree_base"/*; do
      [ -d "$d" ] || continue
      git worktree remove --force "$d" >/dev/null 2>&1 || rm -rf "$d"
    done
    rm -rf "$worktree_base"
  fi
  git worktree prune >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  rm -rf "$pin_dir" 2>/dev/null || true
  rm -f "$sha_file" "$stdin_capture" "$pin_summary"
}
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

# --- ARP-006: resolve the pinned (trusted) copy of each gate script -------
# Pinned once per hook run (not per pushed SHA) — the pin ref does not move
# mid-run, and every pushed SHA is compared against the same trusted baseline.
# prepush-ref-updates.sh is in this list per SEC-005: it runs first, from the
# working tree, and decides which SHAs get gated at all.
#
# ARP-R-05c: keyed on repo-relative PATHS, not bare names under scripts/. The
# old `scripts/${name}` prefix made it structurally impossible to pin anything
# outside that one directory — which is how hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh (curl,
# chmod +x, cargo install), executed by two of the PINNED host test suites,
# stayed outside the pin while the hook reported healthy.
GATE_SCRIPT_PATHS="scripts/run-quality-gate.sh scripts/check-release-parity.sh scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh"
# Subset of the above that this hook never EXECUTES. It is reached transitively:
# check-release-parity.sh globs and runs tests/hooks/test_*.sh, and
# test_no_autoprovision.sh / test_ensure_rally_binary.sh invoke this file out of
# the pushed tree. So it needs the pinned-vs-pushed comparison and the refusal,
# but there is no exec path to choose — nothing here ever picks "run the pinned
# copy instead", because this hook does not run it at all.
GATE_COMPARE_ONLY_PATHS="hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh"

# Pinned copies are stored flat in $pin_dir, so the path separator has to go.
# `__` rather than a bare `_` keeps distinct paths distinct
# (scripts/a__b.sh and scripts/a/b.sh would otherwise collide).
pin_slot() { printf '%s' "$1" | sed 's|/|__|g'; }

if [ -n "${RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF:-}" ]; then
  pin_ref="$RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF"
  pin_ref_source="environment"
else
  pin_ref="main"
  pin_ref_source="default"
fi
pin_commit=""
if git rev-parse --verify --quiet "refs/heads/${pin_ref}^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  pin_commit=$(git rev-parse "refs/heads/${pin_ref}^{commit}")
elif git rev-parse --verify --quiet "${pin_ref}^{commit}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  pin_commit=$(git rev-parse "${pin_ref}^{commit}")
fi

if [ -n "$pin_commit" ]; then
  pin_fetched=0
  for gate_script_path in $GATE_SCRIPT_PATHS; do
    if git cat-file -e "${pin_commit}:${gate_script_path}" 2>/dev/null; then
      git show "${pin_commit}:${gate_script_path}" > "$pin_dir/$(pin_slot "$gate_script_path")" 2>/dev/null
      chmod +x "$pin_dir/$(pin_slot "$gate_script_path")"
      pin_fetched=$((pin_fetched + 1))
    fi
  done
  # ARP-R-05b: NEUTRAL. Copying bytes out of a ref is not review, and this is
  # the only thing that has happened so far. The affirmative statement — which
  # paths were diffed, and which copy ran — is emitted after the last
  # comparison, near the end of this file. Do not restore an affirmative line
  # here: it would be a claim about work that has not run yet.
  echo "pre-push: pin ref '${pin_ref}' (${pin_ref_source}) resolves to ${pin_commit}; fetched ${pin_fetched} pinned gate-script copies — nothing compared yet" >&2
else
  echo "pre-push: WARNING — pin ref '${pin_ref}' does not resolve; gate scripts will run from each pushed tree directly (reduced trust — expected only before '${pin_ref}' exists, e.g. brand-new repo bootstrap)" >&2
fi

# RC-034: hand the resolved pin to the gate scripts so they can extend the pin
# to the entry points they dispatch (check-release-parity.sh uses it to reject
# a tests/hooks/test_*.sh that did not exist at the pin). Assigned from
# $pin_commit unconditionally, and UNSET when nothing pinned: the environment
# is not trusted, so an inherited RALLY_PREPUSH_PIN_COMMIT — which would let a
# caller name a commit that already contains the attacker's test file — must
# never survive into the gate.
if [ -n "$pin_commit" ]; then
  RALLY_PREPUSH_PIN_COMMIT="$pin_commit"
  export RALLY_PREPUSH_PIN_COMMIT
else
  unset RALLY_PREPUSH_PIN_COMMIT
fi

# --- SEC-005: is the pin actually pinning anything? -----------------------
# field 2 of each stdin tuple is local_sha. Read here with awk on purpose —
# see the SEC-005 note in the header for why the parser cannot be trusted to
# answer a question about whether the pin is trustworthy.
pushed_shas_raw=$(awk '{ if ($2 != "" && $2 !~ /^0+$/) print $2 }' "$stdin_capture" | sort -u)

if [ -n "$pin_commit" ] && [ -n "$pushed_shas_raw" ]; then
  pin_is_vacuous=0
  echo "pre-push: pin ${pin_ref} (${pin_ref_source}) @ ${pin_commit}" >&2
  for pushed_sha in $pushed_shas_raw; do
    if [ "$pushed_sha" = "$pin_commit" ]; then
      pin_is_vacuous=1
      echo "pre-push:   pushing ${pushed_sha}  <-- SAME COMMIT AS THE PIN" >&2
    else
      echo "pre-push:   pushing ${pushed_sha}" >&2
    fi
  done

  if [ "$pin_is_vacuous" = "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: WARNING — the pin resolves to a commit being pushed, so the pinned gate scripts and the pushed gate scripts are the same bytes. The pin reviewed nothing on this run." >&2
    if [ "$pin_ref_source" = "environment" ] && [ "${RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN:-}" != "1" ]; then
      echo "pre-push: REFUSED — RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF='${pin_ref}' was supplied from the environment and resolves to a commit in this push. That is exactly how the ARP-006 pin gets silently disabled (SEC-005): set it to HEAD, or to the branch you are pushing, and the pushed tree's own gate scripts execute while the hook reports a healthy pin." >&2
      echo "pre-push: unset RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF to use the default 'main' pin, point it at a ref you actually trust, or — if you genuinely mean to run unpinned — re-run with:" >&2
      echo "pre-push:   RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN=1 git push ..." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    # ARP-R-05a: the DEFAULT pin gets the same refusal as the env pin. This used
    # to warn and continue, on the reasoning that refusing would block every
    # push of the pin branch — but pushing `main` with the pin at `main` IS this
    # repo's normal path, so the warning fired on nearly every push and the
    # gate ran the pushed tree's code regardless. That made the vacuity check a
    # log line on the only path that matters. Same ack as the env case: one
    # deliberate operator decision per push of the pin branch.
    if [ "$pin_ref_source" = "default" ] && [ "${RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN:-}" != "1" ]; then
      echo "pre-push: REFUSED — the default pin '${pin_ref}' resolves to ${pin_commit}, a commit in this push. You are pushing the pin branch itself, so every path in GATE_SCRIPT_PATHS (${GATE_SCRIPT_PATHS}) would be diffed against its own bytes: identical by construction, reviewed by nobody." >&2
      echo "pre-push: this is the repo's ORDINARY path, which is precisely why it is refused rather than warned about (ARP-R-05): a check that passes on every normal push certifies nothing, and the gate still goes on to execute this push's code on this machine." >&2
      echo "pre-push: push a branch and let it be compared against '${pin_ref}', or — if you mean to push '${pin_ref}' itself and accept that its gate scripts were reviewed against no earlier baseline — re-run with:" >&2
      echo "pre-push:   RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN=1 git push ..." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    if [ "$pin_ref_source" = "default" ]; then
      echo "pre-push: continuing — RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN=1 is set and you are pushing the pin branch itself, where the pin cannot be independent. The gate scripts in this push were NOT reviewed against an earlier baseline. Running effectively UNPINNED." >&2
    else
      echo "pre-push: continuing — RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN=1 is set. Running effectively UNPINNED." >&2
    fi
  fi

  # --- RC-034b: every env-supplied pin needs an ack, not just a vacuous one --
  # The vacuity test above is SHA identity, so it only catches a pin aimed at a
  # commit in this push. Aim it at any other ref the attacker controls — a
  # local `evil` branch carrying the same gate scripts as the pushed branch —
  # and the pin resolves, the SHAs differ, `diff -q` reports the pinned and
  # pushed copies identical, and the hook prints an affirmative pin line. The
  # pin's whole claim is "this ref was reviewed", and only an operator can
  # assert that about a ref the environment chose. Reachable in practice: a
  # committed `.envrc` plus one `direnv allow`.
  # RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN=1 satisfies this too — it is the strictly
  # stronger statement ("I accept running effectively unpinned"), and the
  # vacuous branch above has already printed its louder, more specific message.
  if [ "$pin_ref_source" = "environment" ] \
     && [ "${RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_ENV_PIN:-}" != "1" ] \
     && [ "${RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_VACUOUS_PIN:-}" != "1" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: REFUSED — RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF='${pin_ref}' (@ ${pin_commit}) was supplied by the ENVIRONMENT, and the environment is not trusted (SEC-005). A pin only means anything if a human vouched for the ref it names; anything running as this UID can name one — a committed .envrc, a Makefile, an npm script." >&2
    echo "pre-push: the vacuity check above cannot cover this: point the pin at a branch you control that carries the SAME gate scripts as the push, and the SHAs differ, diff -q says identical, and the pin reports healthy having reviewed nothing (RC-034)." >&2
    echo "pre-push: unset RALLY_PREPUSH_GATE_PIN_REF to use the default 'main' pin, or — if you chose '${pin_ref}' deliberately and trust it — re-run with:" >&2
    echo "pre-push:   RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_ENV_PIN=1 git push ..." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
fi

# --- ARP-006 / SEC-005: pinned-vs-candidate decision, one implementation ---
#
# resolve_gate_script SCRIPT_PATH CANDIDATE_PATH LABEL [MODE]
#
# SCRIPT_PATH is repo-relative (ARP-R-05c): scripts/run-quality-gate.sh,
# hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh, … The pinned copy lives at
# "$pin_dir/$(pin_slot SCRIPT_PATH)".
#
# MODE is "exec" (default — this hook is about to run the returned path) or
# "compare" (this hook never runs it; the pinned host tests do). The decision
# table and the refusal are identical; only the wording changes, because
# telling an operator "refusing to execute" about a file this hook does not
# execute would misdescribe the risk they are being asked to accept.
#
# Echoes the path of the copy that should execute. Returns 1 to REFUSE.
#   no pinned copy    -> the candidate (bootstrap; the warning was printed above)
#   identical bytes   -> the pinned copy
#   differs + ack     -> the candidate, after printing the diff
#   differs, no ack   -> refuse, after printing the diff
resolve_gate_script() {
  rgs_path="$1"
  rgs_candidate="$2"
  rgs_label="$3"
  rgs_mode="${4:-exec}"
  rgs_pinned="$pin_dir/$(pin_slot "$rgs_path")"
  if [ ! -f "$rgs_pinned" ]; then
    printf '%s' "$rgs_candidate"
    return 0
  fi
  if diff -q "$rgs_pinned" "$rgs_candidate" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '%s' "$rgs_pinned"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "${RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_GATE_CHANGE:-}" = "1" ]; then
    if [ "$rgs_mode" = "compare" ]; then
      echo "pre-push: $rgs_label — $rgs_path DIFFERS from pinned ${pin_ref} @ ${pin_commit} — RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_GATE_CHANGE=1 is set, the pinned host tests will run the PUSHED version:" >&2
    else
      echo "pre-push: $rgs_label — $rgs_path DIFFERS from pinned ${pin_ref} @ ${pin_commit} — RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_GATE_CHANGE=1 is set, running the PUSHED version:" >&2
    fi
    diff -u "$rgs_pinned" "$rgs_candidate" >&2 || true
    printf '%s' "$rgs_candidate"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "pre-push: REFUSED — $rgs_label's $rgs_path differs from the pinned ${pin_ref} @ ${pin_commit} copy." >&2
  if [ "$rgs_mode" = "compare" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: this hook never dispatches $rgs_path — the PINNED tests/hooks/test_*.sh do (it carries curl, chmod +x and cargo install), so pinning the test files alone left it reachable: every pinned test stays byte-identical, the pin reports healthy, and this file executes unreviewed (ARP-R-05). Refusing by default." >&2
  else
    echo "pre-push: executing an unreviewed, pushed-tree gate script is exactly what ARP-006 closes — refusing by default." >&2
  fi
  echo "pre-push: diff (- pinned ${pin_ref}, + candidate $rgs_label):" >&2
  diff -u "$rgs_pinned" "$rgs_candidate" >&2 || true
  echo "pre-push: if this is a legitimate in-flight edit to the gate itself, review the diff above, then re-run with:" >&2
  echo "pre-push:   RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_GATE_CHANGE=1 git push ..." >&2
  return 1
}

# record_pin_outcome SCRIPT_PATH LABEL EXEC_PATH [MODE]
#
# ARP-R-05b: one line per comparison, appended as the verdict is reached and
# printed together at the end. Derives the verdict from the path
# resolve_gate_script actually returned, so the summary cannot drift from the
# decision it describes.
record_pin_outcome() {
  rpo_path="$1"
  rpo_label="$2"
  rpo_exec="$3"
  rpo_mode="${4:-exec}"
  rpo_pinned="$pin_dir/$(pin_slot "$rpo_path")"
  if [ ! -f "$rpo_pinned" ]; then
    rpo_verdict="NOT PINNED (absent at ${pin_ref:-<unresolved>}) — pushed copy, unreviewed"
  elif [ "$rpo_exec" = "$rpo_pinned" ]; then
    if [ "$rpo_mode" = "compare" ]; then
      rpo_verdict="identical to pin — compared only (run by the pinned host tests, not by this hook)"
    else
      rpo_verdict="identical to pin — ran the PINNED copy"
    fi
  else
    if [ "$rpo_mode" = "compare" ]; then
      rpo_verdict="DIFFERS from pin — pushed copy accepted under RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_GATE_CHANGE=1"
    else
      rpo_verdict="DIFFERS from pin — ran the PUSHED copy under RALLY_PREPUSH_ACK_GATE_CHANGE=1"
    fi
  fi
  echo "pre-push:   ${rpo_path} [${rpo_label}]: ${rpo_verdict}" >> "$pin_summary"
}

# --- run the ref-update parser through the same pin decision (SEC-005) ----
# The parser runs from the WORKING TREE, so its candidate is the working-tree
# copy, not a pushed worktree copy. Everything else is identical.
if ! parser_exec_path=$(resolve_gate_script scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh \
       "$repo_root/scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh" "working tree"); then
  exit 1
fi
record_pin_outcome scripts/prepush-ref-updates.sh "working tree" "$parser_exec_path"
"$parser_exec_path" < "$stdin_capture" > "$sha_file"
rm -f "$stdin_capture"

if [ ! -s "$sha_file" ]; then
  echo "pre-push: no non-deletion refs to validate (pure deletion push, or empty ref-update list) — nothing to gate" >&2
  # ARP-R-05b: the parser comparison above IS a comparison, so report it rather
  # than exiting with the pin's state unstated.
  if [ -s "$pin_summary" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: gate-script pin comparison complete — pinned ${pin_ref} @ ${pin_commit}:" >&2
    cat "$pin_summary" >&2
  fi
  exit 0
fi

# git injects repo-scoping vars (GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE, GIT_INDEX_FILE, …)
# into a hook's environment. Those leak into EVERY `git` subprocess the gate
# spawns: cargo tests that shell out to git (e.g. `git worktree add`,
# `git commit` in temp fixtures) would resolve against the REAL repo instead
# of their own isolated fixtures, and run-quality-gate.sh's own
# `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` could resolve the wrong tree. Strip the
# repo-scoping vars for the gate subshells so the gate runs hermetically
# against the detached worktree it cd'd into. Identity/config vars
# (GIT_AUTHOR_*, GIT_COMMITTER_*, GIT_CONFIG_*) are deliberately left intact.
git_scope_vars='GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE GIT_INDEX_FILE GIT_PREFIX GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY GIT_COMMON_DIR GIT_QUARANTINE_PATH GIT_INTERNAL_GITDIR_HOOK_PATH GIT_REFLOG_ACTION'

# run_gated_script SCRIPT_PATH WORKTREE_DIR
#
# Decides WHICH copy of $SCRIPT_PATH to execute (pinned vs pushed — see the
# ARP-006 header note) and runs it with cwd=$WORKTREE_DIR so the gate's
# SUBJECT is always the pushed tree, regardless of where its CODE came from.
# Returns the gate's own exit status; under `set -e` a non-zero return here
# aborts the whole hook (push blocked), same as before ARP-006.
run_gated_script() {
  gate_script_path="$1"
  gate_wt="$2"
  gate_pushed_script="$gate_wt/$gate_script_path"
  if [ ! -f "$gate_pushed_script" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: $sha — $gate_script_path not present in pushed tree; skipping" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  if ! gate_exec_path=$(resolve_gate_script "$gate_script_path" "$gate_pushed_script" "$sha"); then
    return 1
  fi
  record_pin_outcome "$gate_script_path" "$sha" "$gate_exec_path"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086 # intentional word-split: unset each var name
  ( cd "$gate_wt" && unset $git_scope_vars && "$gate_exec_path" )
}

# compare_gated_script SCRIPT_PATH WORKTREE_DIR
#
# ARP-R-05c. Same pinned-vs-pushed decision and same refusal as
# run_gated_script, minus the exec: these paths are reached transitively (the
# pinned tests/hooks/test_*.sh invoke hooks/ensure-rally-binary.sh out of the
# pushed worktree), so there is nothing for this hook to run — only something
# for it to refuse. Called BEFORE the dispatchers below, so a hostile copy is
# rejected before check-release-parity.sh gets the chance to glob and run the
# tests that would execute it.
compare_gated_script() {
  cmp_script_path="$1"
  cmp_wt="$2"
  cmp_pinned="$pin_dir/$(pin_slot "$cmp_script_path")"
  # Nothing pinned (bootstrap / absent at the pin) — the loud warning was
  # already printed by the pin block; there is no baseline to compare against.
  [ -f "$cmp_pinned" ] || return 0
  cmp_pushed="$cmp_wt/$cmp_script_path"
  if [ ! -f "$cmp_pushed" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: $sha — $cmp_script_path absent from the pushed tree; nothing to compare (it cannot execute either)" >&2
    return 0
  fi
  if ! cmp_exec_path=$(resolve_gate_script "$cmp_script_path" "$cmp_pushed" "$sha" compare); then
    return 1
  fi
  record_pin_outcome "$cmp_script_path" "$sha" "$cmp_exec_path" compare
  return 0
}

sha_count=0
while IFS= read -r sha; do
  [ -n "$sha" ] || continue
  sha_count=$((sha_count + 1))
  wt="$worktree_base/$sha"
  echo "pre-push: $sha — creating clean detached worktree…" >&2
  git worktree add --detach "$wt" "$sha" >/dev/null
  for cmp_path in $GATE_COMPARE_ONLY_PATHS; do
    echo "pre-push: $sha — comparing $cmp_path against the pin (executed by the pinned host tests, not by this hook)…" >&2
    compare_gated_script "$cmp_path" "$wt"
  done
  echo "pre-push: $sha — running quality gate (scripts/run-quality-gate.sh)…" >&2
  run_gated_script scripts/run-quality-gate.sh "$wt"
  echo "pre-push: $sha — running release parity (scripts/check-release-parity.sh)…" >&2
  # f2: parity uses RALLY_CODEX_DEST (SEC-003) to regenerate the Codex
  # artifact into a scratch dir, so this never mutates the detached
  # worktree it runs in.
  run_gated_script scripts/check-release-parity.sh "$wt"
  echo "pre-push: $sha ✅ gate + parity passed" >&2
done < "$sha_file"

# --- ARP-R-05b: the affirmative pin statement, AFTER the last comparison ----
# Every line below is a verdict that was already reached above. This is the
# only place the hook is entitled to assert anything about the pin, and it
# asserts exactly what was diffed against what — not "pinned", which reads as
# a guarantee about code the pin never saw.
if [ -s "$pin_summary" ]; then
  echo "pre-push: gate-script pin comparison complete — pinned ${pin_ref} @ ${pin_commit}:" >&2
  cat "$pin_summary" >&2
elif [ -n "$pin_commit" ]; then
  echo "pre-push: gate-script pin ${pin_ref} @ ${pin_commit} resolved, but NO path was compared against it on this run." >&2
else
  echo "pre-push: no gate-script pin was in effect on this run — every gate script came from the pushed tree, unreviewed." >&2
fi

# Not "all gates green": the two dispatchers exiting 0 is all that was
# established. Everything they compiled and ran out of the pushed tree ran
# unpinned, and a blanket all-clear here is what let the earlier affirmative
# line be read as coverage it never had (ARP-R-05b).
echo "pre-push: gated ${sha_count} pushed SHA(s) — scripts/run-quality-gate.sh and scripts/check-release-parity.sh each exited 0 on every one." >&2
echo "pre-push: that verdict covers those two dispatchers only. The pushed-tree code they compiled and executed — cargo test bodies, build.rs, proc macros, scripts/generate_host_surfaces.py, tests/scripts/*.py, scripts/build-codex-artifact.sh, the globbed tests/hooks/test_*.sh — ran on this machine and is NOT covered by the pin. See the header note." >&2
